<article class="env-remark" id="431C53">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/431C53">Remark <span data-tag="431C53">5.5.6.12</span>.</a> A morphism $f: C \rightarrow D$ in an $\infty $-category $\calC $ is $k$-truncated if and only if it is $k$-truncated when regarded as an object of the $\infty $-category $\calC ^{/D}$ (since the natural map $\calC _{/D} \rightarrow \calC ^{/D}$ is an equivalence of $\infty $-categories). We may identify $\calC ^{/D}$ with $p^{-1} \{ D \} $, where $p$ denotes the evaluation map $\calC ^{\Delta ^1} \rightarrow \calC ^{\{ 1\} }$. Corollary <a href="/tag/20018F" data-tag="20018F">2.4.7.12</a> implies that $p$ is a coCartesian fibration. Consequently, Remark <a href="/tag/951E95" data-tag="951E95">5.5.6.7</a> translates into the following assertion: if </p>
  <div class="equation">
  \[  \xymatrix { C' \ar [r]^{f'} \ar [d] &amp;  D' \ar [d] \\ C \ar [r]^{f} &amp;  D } \]
</div>
  <p> is a pullback diagram in $\calC $, and $f$ is $k$-truncated, then $f'$ is $k$-truncated. </p>
</article>